hello and welcome to the Monte Carlo  simulation experiment with Python poor  we began we should establish what a  Monte Carlo simulation is the idea of a  Monte Carlo simulation is to test  various outcome possibilities so in  reality only one of the outcome  possibilities will actually play out but  in terms of risk assessment any of the  possibilities could have occurred and  therefore should should be weighed in to  whether or not a decision was a good one  so Monte Carlo simulators are often used  to assess risk of a given say trading  strategy with options or stocks Monte  Carlo simulators can help drive the  point home that success is success is  not the only measure of whether a choice  was good or not  choices should not be assessed after  their outcome instead the risks and  benefits should only be considered at  the time the decision was made so the  whole hindsight bias you know we have a  human's just in general are very good at  fitting you know reasoning to why  something might have occurred after the  fact but we're pretty bad at it before  the before the fact so a Monte Carlo  simulator can help help someone  visualize most or all in some scenarios  of the potential outcomes to have a much  better idea regarding the risk of a  decision so with that let's consider in  this video in the next few videos a  really basic example so here we're gonna  consider a gambling scenario where a  user can role quote unquote a  metaphorical dice for an outcome of  something like 1 to 100 if the user  rolls anything from a 1 to a 50 the  quote unquote house wins if the user  rolls anything from a 51 to a 99 the  quote unquote user wins if the user  rolls a 100 they lose so with this the  house maintains a mere 1% edge which is  markedly lower than the  in most situations where you have  speculation or gambling taking place so  for example consider if you're trading  with say Scottrade where the  metaphorical house is taking seven  dollars a trade if you invest a tho